//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Draft Styling  (shared by all three draft screens)
//
// ONE RULE: a card is never removed from the board.
//
// Every screen used to hide an option the moment it was banned or taken. That
// made the board shrink and reflow under the players - options jumped rows
// mid-draft, and a player who looked away for two seconds could not tell
// whether a unit had been banned, taken by the opponent, or was simply never
// in the pool. Banned and taken cards now STAY where they are, restyled and
// unclickable, so the board is the same shape from the first click to the last
// and reads as a record of what happened to it.
//
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY EVERY CARD SETS <s val="..."> EXPLICITLY
//
// The default label style on a trigger dialog button is StandardButton
// (core.sc2mod, UI/FontStyles.SC2Style):
//
//   <Style name="StandardButton" template="HeaderExtendedTemplate" height="18"
//          styleflags="Shadow|Uppercase" disabledcolor="#ColorStandardButtonDisabled" .../>
//
// Three separate problems came out of that one line, all of them visible in a
// playtest screenshot of the modifier draft:
//
//   1. disabledcolor (#ColorStandardButtonDisabled = 3d5d7a) is applied to the
//      WHOLE label the moment the button is disabled, and it overrides every
//      inline <c val> in the string. Since a claimed card is a disabled button,
//      banned / yours / theirs all rendered in the same flat blue-grey - the
//      colour channel was not weak, it was being thrown away outright.
//   2. styleflags="Uppercase" forced every name into ALL CAPS.
//   3. height=18 in FontHeaderExtended - a wide display face - made the text so
//      large that names truncated ("Veteran Forces" -> "Veter...") and the
//      second line fell outside the 56px button and never drew at all. Every
//      marker that lived on line two, including the older BAN marker, had in
//      fact never been visible.
//
// The fix is to replace the style on the control itself, via
// c_triggerControlPropertyStyle (this is what the editor's "Set Dialog Item
// Style" action does - libNtve_gf_SetDialogItemStyle in NativeLib.galaxy).
// StandardButtonMini is the one stock style that fixes all three at once:
//
//   <Style name="StandardButtonMini" template="StandardTemplate" height="16"
//          vjustify="Middle" hjustify="Center" styleflags="Glow" .../>
//
// No disabledcolor, so inline <c val> survives being disabled. No Uppercase.
// StandardTemplate's narrower font at height 16, so names fit and both lines
// draw. And it keeps the same centring StandardButton had, so replacing the
// style does not move anything on screen. The detail line additionally sets
// Standard12 inline, purely to make it smaller than the name.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A card's state is carried by FIVE redundant channels, so no single one of
// them has to be trusted:
//
//   state        line 1        line 2                        frame
//   OPEN         Overwatch     Allies near a fight...        lit, clickable
//   BAN TARGET   Overwatch     BAN · Allies near a...        lit, clickable
//   BANNED       Overwatch     [X] BANNED · Allies...        flat
//   YOURS / P1   Overwatch     [+] YOURS · Allies...         flat
//   THEIRS / P2  Overwatch     [O] THEIRS · Allies...        flat
//
//   1. A WORD. BANNED / YOURS / THEIRS / TAKEN is spelled out. Nothing has to
//      be inferred from an appearance at all.
//   2. A GLYPH. [X], [+], [O], [=] are four different SHAPES in a fixed
//      leading position on line two, so they hold a scannable column.
//   3. THE BUTTON FRAME. A claimed card is disabled, so SC2 drops its lit blue
//      frame to a flat panel. Which options are still live reads from the shape
//      of the grid alone, with no text read and no colour seen.
//   4. BRIGHTNESS. Banned sits below anything still on offer, taken between the
//      two. This ordering survives greyscale.
//   5. COLOUR, both as text and as a tint on the button itself. It is never the
//      load-bearing channel. Green/orange for yours/theirs is precisely the
//      pair a deuteranope cannot separate - which is why that pair also differs
//      by word, by glyph and by brightness.
//
// The name stays alone on line one, flush left and unabbreviated: what a card
// IS is read far more often than what happened to it, and a chip in front of
// the name cost the end of every long name to say something line two already
// said. BAN sits in the same slot as the state chips because it is the same
// kind of information at the same moment - what this card is to you right now -
// and a ban target is never also banned, so the two can never collide.
//
// Keeping the detail on a dead card (dimmed) rather than blanking it is
// deliberate: the line keeps its height, so nothing below it moves when a card
// changes state.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Card states.
const int c_draftStateOpen      = 0; // on offer; clicking picks it
const int c_draftStateBanTarget = 1; // on offer; clicking BANS it
const int c_draftStateBanned    = 2; // removed by a ban
const int c_draftStateMine      = 3; // taken by the side this viewer is on
const int c_draftStateTheirs    = 4; // taken by the other side
const int c_draftStateTaken     = 5; // claimed, no side (shared-pool drafts)

// Text styles - see the block above for why these are set at all. All three are
// plain StandardTemplate styles from core.sc2mod, so they are available to
// every mod without shipping a style file.
const string c_draftStyleButton = "StandardButtonMini"; // the button control
const string c_draftStyleBody   = "Standard12";         // chip + detail, line 2
const string c_draftStyleTiny   = "Standard11";         // legends and headers

// Chip glyphs. Four distinct shapes, bracketed so they hold a fixed-width
// column down the left edge of line two; all plain ASCII, so nothing here
// depends on a font that may not have the character.
const string c_draftGlyphBanned = "[X]";
const string c_draftGlyphMine   = "[+]";
const string c_draftGlyphTheirs = "[O]";
const string c_draftGlyphTaken  = "[=]";

// Separator between a chip and the card detail. Same middle dot the rest of
// the draft text already uses.
const string c_draftDot = "·";

// Legend lines, in the same glyph order as the board. The short one is for a
// header that already carries other text; the long one for a line of its own.
const string c_draftLegendShort =
    "<c val=\"C08888\">[X] BANNED</c>   <c val=\"4ADB92\">[+] YOURS / P1</c>   "
    + "<c val=\"E8913F\">[O] THEIRS / P2</c>";
const string c_draftLegend =
    c_draftLegendShort + "   <c val=\"7A7A7A\">- nothing leaves the board</c>";

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The colours and the chip for one state.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
string DraftStyle_NameColor(int state) {
    if (state == c_draftStateBanned) { return "B07878"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateMine)   { return "4ADB92"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateTheirs) { return "F0A050"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateTaken)  { return "9FB4C7"; }
    return "FFFFFF";
}

string DraftStyle_BodyColor(int state) {
    if (state == c_draftStateBanTarget) { return "FF8080"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateBanned)    { return "8A5E5E"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateMine)      { return "3FB077"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateTheirs)    { return "B5793A"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateTaken)     { return "6F8496"; }
    return "8FC4E8";
}

// A tint multiplied over the button image itself, so a claimed card reads as
// claimed before a single character is read. This is a second, independent
// path for the colour: even if a style override ever swallows the inline text
// colours again, the button underneath still carries the state.
//
// Only claimed states have one. An option still on offer is reset to white (no
// tint), so it keeps SC2's stock button exactly as the rest of the UI draws it.
// The reset matters: a control is not tied to one card for the whole draft.
color DraftStyle_Tint(int state) {
    if (state == c_draftStateBanned) { return Color(60, 32, 32); }
    if (state == c_draftStateMine)   { return Color(34, 78, 54); }
    if (state == c_draftStateTheirs) { return Color(80, 52, 24); }
    return Color(52, 60, 68);
}

bool DraftStyle_HasTint(int state) {
    return state == c_draftStateBanned || state == c_draftStateMine
        || state == c_draftStateTheirs || state == c_draftStateTaken;
}

// The chip that leads a claimed card's detail line - glyph plus word. tag
// overrides the default word: spectators are on neither side, so they get
// "P1"/"P2" instead of "YOURS"/"THEIRS". A card still on offer has no chip,
// so its detail line starts flush with its description.
string DraftStyle_Chip(int state, string tag) {
    string word;
    word = tag;
    if (state == c_draftStateBanTarget) { return "BAN"; }
    if (state == c_draftStateBanned) {
        if (word == "") { word = "BANNED"; }
        return c_draftGlyphBanned + " " + word;
    }
    if (state == c_draftStateMine) {
        if (word == "") { word = "YOURS"; }
        return c_draftGlyphMine + " " + word;
    }
    if (state == c_draftStateTheirs) {
        if (word == "") { word = "THEIRS"; }
        return c_draftGlyphTheirs + " " + word;
    }
    if (state == c_draftStateTaken) {
        if (word == "") { word = "TAKEN"; }
        return c_draftGlyphTaken + " " + word;
    }
    return "";
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A full two-line card: the name, then the chip and the detail. Pass tag "" for
// the default word for the state. Separators are dropped when either half is
// missing, so a pool entry with no blurb doesn't end on a dangling dot.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
string DraftStyle_Card(int state, string tag, string name, string detail) {
    string chip;
    string body;

    chip = DraftStyle_Chip(state, tag);
    if (chip == "")        { body = detail; }
    else if (detail == "") { body = chip; }
    else                   { body = chip + " " + c_draftDot + " " + detail; }

    // The name line takes the control's own style (see the header): leaving it
    // unwrapped is what keeps it centred like every other button in the mod.
    return "<c val=\"" + DraftStyle_NameColor(state) + "\">" + name + "</c>"
        + "\n<s val=\"" + c_draftStyleBody + "\"><c val=\"" + DraftStyle_BodyColor(state)
        + "\">" + body + "</c></s>";
}

// Wrap a header or legend in the small style, so it fits the width it was given
// instead of being truncated by the default display font.
string DraftStyle_Small(string s) {
    return "<s val=\"" + c_draftStyleTiny + "\">" + s + "</s>";
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call once per draft button, right after DialogControlCreate. This is the
// call that actually restores the colours on a disabled card - see the header
// block for what the stock StandardButton style was doing to them.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
void DraftStyle_InitButton(int control, playergroup viewer) {
    DialogControlSetPropertyAsString(control, c_triggerControlPropertyStyle, viewer,
        c_draftStyleButton);
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Apply the whole treatment for one card to one viewer: text, tint, visibility
// and whether it can be clicked. Every screen goes through this, so none of
// them can style a card one way and gate it another. canClick is the viewer's
// own right to act (it is false for the waiting player and for spectators);
// the card's state decides the rest.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
void DraftStyle_ApplyCard(int control, playergroup viewer, int state, string card, bool canClick) {
    DialogControlSetPropertyAsText(control, c_triggerControlPropertyText, viewer,
        StringToText(card));
    // Set both ways too: the same control is reused for different cards (the
    // roster draft swaps between the two players' pools), so a tint left over
    // from the previous card would otherwise bleed onto an untouched one.
    if (DraftStyle_HasTint(state)) {
        DialogControlSetPropertyAsColor(control, c_triggerControlPropertyColor, viewer,
            DraftStyle_Tint(state));
    } else {
        DialogControlSetPropertyAsColor(control, c_triggerControlPropertyColor, viewer,
            Color(100, 100, 100));
    }
    DialogControlSetVisible(control, viewer, true);
    // Set both ways: a button disabled on this player's waiting turn would
    // otherwise stay disabled on their own turn.
    DialogControlSetEnabled(control, viewer,
        canClick && (state == c_draftStateOpen || state == c_draftStateBanTarget));
}

